COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the photograph fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the frame height contains nothing but the empty scuffed vinyl floor of the branch hall running toward the camera, dark and free of detail so overlay text stays readable on it. Every face, hand, phone, cup and screen sits in the upper five-eighths, arranged in three clear horizontal bands: the wide display wall runs across the very top of the frame, the row of standing and seated customers sits directly beneath it in the middle band, and the right-hand man's chest and shoulders close the middle band on the right. Chair backs and the counter ledge run across the MIDDLE of the frame, never near the bottom edge. Frame the right-hand man from the chest up and place his head high, close to the top edge. Scene: photographed inside the public customer hall of a Korean securities branch late on a weekday afternoon, camera standing on the floor at the back of the hall and tilted slightly upward so it looks past the heads of the crowd toward the display wall — the viewpoint stays behind and below them, never above. Across the full width of the top of the frame is a wall of joined flat monitors; the largest panel carries one single steep blue line falling almost vertically off the right edge of its own chart area, with thin blue bars stacked beneath it, and the smaller side panels hold only soft columns of colour bars that are clearly out of focus. The screens show chart lines and colour bars only — no numerals, no tickers, no invented lettering of any kind. Filling the middle band on the left and centre, six or seven Korean customers in their sixties and seventies stand and sit close together facing the wall: one has both hands laced on top of his head, one presses a flat palm over his own mouth, one holds a folding phone to his ear with his shoulders drawn up, one has taken his glasses off and is pinching the bridge of his nose, and none of them is speaking to another. A steel-framed waiting bench, a stainless water cup left on its armrest, a folded newspaper, and a taped paper notice on a pillar with small Korean handwriting sit around them as out-of-focus texture, legibility not required. On the right third of the frame, chest up and much larger than the crowd, stands a Korean man in his sixties in a zip-up outdoor jacket with a knitted collar, thinning grey hair pushed back; he is the only one not looking at the wall — his eyes are half-lowered and aimed just past the camera, the lids heavy, his mouth set in a flat closed line with the jaw slack rather than clenched, one hand still holding a rolled-up newspaper against his chest and the other hanging dead at his side. Light is honest and mixed: flat white ceiling fluorescents over the hall, a colder blue wash coming off the monitor wall onto the upturned faces below it, and low afternoon sun through the street glass behind the camera falling only on the right-hand man, so the frame reads as a lit top band, a shadowed crowd, and one lit face at small size. Style: candid documentary photograph of a real place, not an advertisement, not CGI, not a composite. Shot on a full-frame camera with a 35mm lens at about f/2.8, standing eye level, available light only, true-to-life restrained color, mild sensor noise, a slight handheld tilt, honest texture on skin, fabric, plastic, glass and painted steel. Signs may clip a little but the overall image stays natural — never glowing, never hyper-saturated, no HDR look, no rim-light effects, no lens flare. No title overlay text, no captions, no subtitle bars, no large lettering across the frame, no watermark, no arrows, no highlight circles, no added graphic overlay of any kind.

COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the photograph fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the frame height contains nothing but the bare polished floor of the hall running toward the camera, dark and almost featureless so overlay text stays readable on it. Every face, hand, phone, bag and screen sits in the upper five-eighths in three horizontal bands: the monitor wall across the very top, the standing crowd in the middle band, and the right-hand woman's head and shoulders anchoring the middle band on the right. Handbag straps, a counter ledge and a rope stanchion run across the MIDDLE of the frame, never near the bottom edge. Frame the right-hand woman from the chest up and place her head high, close to the top edge. Scene: photographed inside the same Korean securities branch hall after closing hours, most of the ceiling lights switched off so the room is lit mainly by its own screens, camera standing on the floor toward the back and tilted slightly upward past the heads of the remaining crowd — the viewpoint stays behind and below them, never above. Along the full width of the top of the frame the monitor wall is still on: one dominant panel shows a single blue line dropping steeply and running off the bottom of its chart area, flanked by dim columns of blue and grey bars that fall away out of focus. The screens carry chart lines and colour bars only — no numerals, no tickers, no invented lettering. In the middle band, five or six people stand scattered rather than orderly: two have their backs half-turned to the wall with phones pressed hard to their ears and free hands cupped over the mouthpiece, one older man stands with both arms hanging and his chin tipped up at the screens, a younger staff member in a company vest is frozen mid-step with a stack of paper against her hip. A tipped-over paper cup, a chair pulled out of line, an unplugged floor lamp and a laminated notice taped to a pillar with small Korean lettering sit around them as texture, legibility not required. On the right third of the frame, chest up and clearly the largest figure, is a Korean woman in her sixties in a quilted coat with a scarf still knotted at her throat, short permed hair; her eyes are stretched wide with the whites showing under the irises and locked on the falling line, her eyebrows are pulled up and squeezed together, the back of one hand is pressed flat against her own mouth while the other clutches the strap of a shoulder bag at chest height, and her chin has drawn back toward her neck. Light comes only from the screens and one warm service lamp at the far counter, so the top band and the woman's face are lit cold blue while the crowd between them sinks into heavy shadow — at thumbnail size the image separates into a bright top strip, a dark middle, and one lit face on the right. Style: candid documentary photograph of a real place, not an advertisement, not CGI, not a composite. Shot on a full-frame camera with a 35mm lens at about f/2.8, standing eye level, available light only, true-to-life restrained color, mild sensor noise, a slight handheld tilt, honest texture on skin, wool, plastic, glass and painted metal. Signs may clip a little but the overall image stays natural — never glowing, never hyper-saturated, no HDR look, no rim-light effects, no lens flare. No title overlay text, no captions, no subtitle bars, no large lettering across the frame, no watermark, no arrows, no highlight circles, no added graphic overlay of any kind.

COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the photograph fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the frame height contains nothing but the bare varnished wooden floor of the room running toward the camera, shadowed and free of detail so overlay text stays readable on it. Every face, hand, cup, remote and screen sits in the upper five-eighths in three horizontal bands: the wall-mounted television across the very top, the seated row of older people in the middle band, and the right-hand man's head and shoulders closing the middle band on the right. The low floor table and the cushions run across the MIDDLE of the frame, never near the bottom edge, and anything resting on them sits at mid height. Frame the right-hand man from the chest up and place his head high, close to the top edge. Scene: photographed inside a modest Korean senior community room in the late afternoon, camera set low near the floor at the back and tilted slightly upward past the seated heads toward the television — the viewpoint stays behind and below them, never above. Mounted high on the wall across the top of the frame is a wide flat television showing a market news broadcast: one steep blue line falling toward the lower right of its own chart area with a block of blue bars beneath it. The screen shows the chart line and colour bars only — no numerals, no tickers, no invented lettering. In the middle band, five or six Korean men and women in their seventies sit close together on floor cushions facing the screen, none of them speaking: one holds a paper cup of barley tea halfway to his mouth and has stopped, one has both hands resting palm-up on her knees, one leans forward with his forearms on the low table and his forehead creased, and one has turned her face away from the screen entirely. Around them sit an aluminium tea kettle, a pile of folded newspapers, a worn TV remote with the rubber buttons rubbed blank, a wall calendar and a strip of Korean handwriting taped beside the door, all small and out of focus as texture, legibility not required. On the right third of the frame, chest up and larger than everyone else, is a Korean man in his seventies in a grey cardigan over a checked shirt, deep lines at his mouth; he has taken his glasses off and holds them loosely at chest height in one hand while the other hand rubs the bridge of his nose, his eyes are narrowed almost shut with a deep vertical crease cut between his brows, and his lips are pushed forward and pressed together as if he has just decided not to say something. Light is late afternoon sun coming through a paper-screened window on the left, warm and low across the seated group, mixed with the cold blue spill of the television above them, so the top band reads cold, the seated middle sits in warm shadow, and the right-hand face catches the window light — three clearly separated brightness blocks at small size. Style: candid documentary photograph of a real place, not an advertisement, not CGI, not a composite. Shot on a full-frame camera with a 35mm lens at about f/2.8, low seated eye level, available light only, true-to-life restrained color, mild sensor noise, a slight handheld tilt, honest texture on skin, wool, paper, aluminium and varnished wood. Signs may clip a little but the overall image stays natural — never glowing, never hyper-saturated, no HDR look, no rim-light effects, no lens flare. No title overlay text, no captions, no subtitle bars, no large lettering across the frame, no watermark, no arrows, no highlight circles, no added graphic overlay of any kind.
